BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/the-ritual-of-belonging-35-years-of-the-friday-center"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261031T051649Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54115387065565
DTSTART:20261105T233000Z
DTEND:20261106T014500Z
DESCRIPTION:Come. Connect. Celebrate.\n\n \n\nIn 1991\, UNC President Willi
 am C. Friday opened the doors to what he called "the citizens' classroom\,
 " a place built to make learning and connection open to everyone\, with or
  without a UNC degree. Named for Bill and Ida Friday\, two North Carolinia
 ns defined by their service to education and public life\, the Friday Cent
 er was founded on a simple belief: learning never stops.\n\nFor 35 years\,
  the Friday Center has carried that belief forward by opening doors\, brin
 ging people together\, and extending Carolina's knowledge beyond the tradi
 tional classroom. It is a place where the University and the community mee
 t\, where ideas are shared\, relationships are built\, and learning become
 s part of a larger public life.\n\nThis November\, we're celebrating 35 ye
 ars with an evening centered on something that has always been essential t
 o the Friday Center: the power of gathering\, and the belonging it creates
 .\n\nJoining us is Casper ter Kuile\, author of The Power of Ritual and co
 -founder of Sacred Design Lab.\n\n \n\nTHE POWER OF RITUAL\n\nAmidst risin
 g rates of disconnection\, what are the rituals that gather us together? H
 ow can we deepen our relationships\, make meaningful connections\, and exp
 erience joyful belonging? Join author and co-founder of Sacred Design Lab 
 Casper ter Kuile for an evening's conversation on the power of ritual. You
 'll explore how to cultivate the containers of connection at home\, on cam
 pus\, and in the wider community. Learn how ritual can weave us back into 
 a fabric of common life.\n\n \n\nThe Evening\n\n6:30pm | Director's recept
 ion\, join us for refreshments and conversation\n7:15pm | Casper's talk\, 
 followed by a live Q&A\n\n \n\nThis is a free event\, but space is limited
 \, and registration is required to attend.
GEO:35.899895;-79.012764
LOCATION:William and Ida Friday Center
SUMMARY:The Ritual of Belonging: 35 Years of the Friday Center
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/the-ritual-of-belonging-35-yea
 rs-of-the-friday-center
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events 1 Ics": "https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/mecklenburgs-declaration-ncs-revolutionary-presbyterians"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_1_ics
X-SOURCE:NC Cultural Resources
DTSTAMP:20261031T052604Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53178468615731
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261106
DESCRIPTION:An article published in the April 30\, 1819 edition of the Rale
 igh Register and North Carolina Gazette proclaimed the following: “It is
  not probably known to many of our readers\, that the citizens of Mecklenb
 urg County\, in this State\, made a Declaration of Independence more than 
 a year before Congress made theirs. The following Document on the subject 
 has lately come to the hands of the Editor from unquestionable authority\,
   and is published that it may go down to posterity.”  \n\nThe document 
 in question was called the Mecklenburg Declaration of Independence\, and i
 t had allegedly declared Mecklenburg “free and independent” from Briti
 sh rule on May 20\, 1775. Of the 27 men who were said to have signed the d
 ocument\, almost all were Presbyterian.\n\nBut many people were confused 
 – why were they just now hearing about this revolutionary action? The or
 iginal document burned in a house fire in 1800\, and the published version
  had been reconstructed from notes made by the clerk of the 1775 meeting. 
 No extant copies could be found.\n\nThe debate over the authenticity of th
 e Mecklenburg Declaration began. And\, when a copy of a document called th
 e Mecklenburg Resolves written on May 30\, 1775\, was discovered in a 1775
  newspaper\, the debate intensified. The Resolves were revolutionary\, but
  stopped short of declaring independence. Did both documents exist? Or was
  the Declaration being conflated with the Resolves?\n\nExamine the evidenc
 e and decide for yourself by visiting the new exhibit\, “Mecklenburg Dec
 laration?” at the Presbyterian Heritage Center.\n\nThe PHC is open Tuesd
 ay-Friday (10-4) and Saturday (12-4).
GEO:35.646737;-82.300463
LOCATION:Presbyterian Heritage Center
SUMMARY:Mecklenburg's Declaration: NC's Revolutionary Presbyterians
URL;VALUE=URI:https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/mecklenburgs-declaration-ncs
 -revolutionary-presbyterians
CATEGORIES:Exhibits & Tours
CATEGORIES:Exhibitions
END:VEVENT
