BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/unc-postdoctoral-associations-research-cafe-8473"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261031T051650Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54318856079110
DTSTART:20261110T230000Z
DTEND:20261111T000000Z
DESCRIPTION:Have you ever noticed how much hotter a parking lot feels than 
 a tree-lined street on a summer day? Even within the same city\, temperatu
 res can vary dramatically over surprisingly short distances. But what caus
 es these differences\, and what can we do to make our cities cooler? Citie
 s are often hotter than their rural surroundings\, a phenomenon known as t
 he urban heat island effect. As climate change brings rising temperatures 
 and more frequent heatwaves\, understanding how heat varies within cities 
 is becoming increasingly important.\n\n \n\nIn this talk\, Dr. Berk will l
 ead us to explore multitudes of factors that influence urban temperatures\
 , using her findings from right here in Durham and Raleigh. She will also 
 share what we can do about urban heat: from planting trees to installing r
 eflective pavements\, which strategies actually work\, and how can science
  help us create cooler\, healthier cities.\n\n \n\nJoin us in Chapel Hill 
 Public Library Meeting Room A for this exciting talk! Light refreshment wi
 ll be provided.
GEO:35.932032;-79.035632
LOCATION:Chapel Hill Public Library\, Meeting Room A
SUMMARY:UNC Postdoctoral Association's Research Cafe
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/unc-postdoctoral-associations-
 research-cafe-8473
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
