BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Triangleland": "https://triangleland.org/event/biomimicry-nature-as-a-mentor"}
X-SOURCE-ID:triangleland
X-SOURCE:Triangle Land Conservancy
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261117T093000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261117T110000
DTSTAMP:20261005T023532Z
CREATED:20261005T023532Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261005T023532Z
UID:10000122-1794907800-1794913200@triangleland.org
SUMMARY:Biomimicry: Nature as a Mentor
DESCRIPTION:Learn about biomimicry at Swift Creek Bluffs!\nBiomimicry (from the Greek bios\, meaning life\, and mimesis\, meaning to imitate) is the practice of looking at how plants\, animals\, and ecosystems solve problems and then copying those designs to solve human challenges. It’s not about using nature as a resource (like cutting down trees for wood); it’s about learning from nature as a mentor. \n\nJoin Hike Leader\, Leigh Aultman\, for a morning of Biological Storytelling. We will walk the short creek loop trail with up to six stops where stories will be shared about local organisms' biological adaptations and how those adaptations and innovations can inform human designs.  This is a free event\, but preregistration is required. Due to the size of the parking lot\, we will keep the hiking group small and ask that you register by vehicle rather than individual. Feel free to bring guests but please carpool to ensure we have enough space in the parking lot.
URL:https://triangleland.org/event/biomimicry-nature-as-a-mentor
LOCATION:Swift Creek Bluffs Nature Preserve\, 7800 Holly Springs Rd\, Raleigh\, NC\, 27606\, United States
ORGANIZER;CN="Amaya Horner":MAILTO:ahorner@triangleland.org
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Gardens": "https://gardens.duke.edu/event/the-story-of-stone-in-the-culberson-asiatic-arboretum/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:gardens
X-SOURCE:Sarah P. Duke Gardens
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261117T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261117T120000
DTSTAMP:20260915T130805Z
CREATED:20260706T123920Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260915T130805Z
UID:17508-1794909600-1794916800@gardens.duke.edu
SUMMARY:The Story of Stone in the Culberson Asiatic Arboretum
DESCRIPTION:Join Paul Jones\, founding curator of the Culberson Asiatic Arboretum\, for a walking tour of some of the most iconic stone features found in the arboretum. You’ll hear the stories behind the stone\, including traditional stone lanterns\, pathways\, waterfalls and lichen-covered boulders that blend seamlessly into the landscape. Hear why specific boulders were chosen and how they were placed\, and learn more about the design intent. You’ll discover how a single carefully positioned rock can anchor an entire landscape\, and how the interplay of stone\, water and plants creates a sense of harmony that feels both timeless and deeply intentional. \nFee: $22; free for Duke Gardens members with discount code. \nParking: Parking fees apply.\n \nLocation: Meet at the Barnes Welcome Center.\n \nAccessibility: This will be a small group program that takes place outdoors\, traveling on a combination of paved and unpaved paths with some uneven surfaces and moderate inclines.
URL:https://gardens.duke.edu/event/the-story-of-stone-in-the-culberson-asiatic-arboretum/
LOCATION:Duke Gardens–Barnes Welcome Center\, 420 Anderson Street\, Durham\, NC\, 27705\, United States
CATEGORIES:Adult Programs
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://gardens.duke.edu/wp-content/uploads/aa_Great-Pond_Rocks_LSullivan_0022_082622-scaled.jpg
END:VEVENT
